  "projectUrl": "https://github.com/Thorium/fsharp-refactor",
  "published": "2026-11-17T11:59:24.75Z",
  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "0.8.56: Reads code with FSharp.Compiler.Service 43.13.404 (FSharp.Analyzers.SDK 0.43), the compiler of FsAutoComplete 0.87.\n\n 0.8.55: Multi-targeted projects are swept narrowest API first by version (net462 before net47, .NET Framework below 4.6.1 before netstandard2.0); a solution without Any CPU builds for x64 or its first platform; redirected output is UTF-8. Analyzer fixes as in the analyzers package.\n\n 0.8.54: --exclude-codes leaves rules out of a run, after --codes and --categories and ahead of fsharprefactor.json; the MCP analyze call takes excludeCodes.\n\n 0.8.53: A compilation that finished keeps its fixes when Ctrl+C or a file error comes before the next framework's pass. Ctrl+C exits 130, never a stack trace. An empty or comment-only fsharprefactor.json refuses nothing. --mcp answers a refused call with its own reason. A generator's banner is read at the end of a header line and below an attribute of several lines. A glob ignores case on macOS.\n\n 0.8.52: Reads code with FSharp.Compiler.Service 43.12.402 (FSharp.Analyzers.SDK 0.41), the compiler of FsAutoComplete 0.86.\n\n 0.8.51:A run that fails or is interrupted (Ctrl+C) puts back the compilation in progress; a source that cannot be written costs that compilation, not a crash. A fsharprefactor.json that is no JSON, a --report into a missing directory, `--report --dry-run` and filters that leave no rule are refused before anything is written. A glob searches the directories it names (`src/App*/*.fsproj` swept all of `src`). A single named file gets no --api-changes reshapes. --mcp works inside the directory it is given or started in and writes nothing outside it, applies nothing under --dry-run, validates `codes` and `categories` and survives a malformed request. Prose such as \"types generated by JsonProvider\" no longer marks a file generated.",
